<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nonlinear attitude stabilization.</span> We also consider a nonlinear attitude stabilization task,
where we drive the attitude rates of a rigid-body <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\omega\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\omega\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (rad/s) to zero by actuating the torques <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The nonlinear continuous-time system dynamics are</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J\dot{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J\dot{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=J\omega\times\omega+\tau," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=J\omega\times\omega+\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal inertia matrix. We discretize the system using a forward Euler scheme with a time step of <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> s. We consider an MPC policy with a prediction horizon <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=25</annotation></semantics></math> and costs defined as in <span id="A2.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QP</span> with nominal time-invariant cost matrices <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=R=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=R=I</annotation></semantics></math>. The learned parameters are defined as <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=(\mathrm{diag}(Q),\mathrm{diag}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=(\mathrm{diag}(Q),\mathrm{diag}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>, and we train these parameters via gradient descent on the RL reward <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,u)=-(0.1\|x\|_{2}^{2}+\|u\|_{2}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,u)=-(0.1\|x\|_{2}^{2}+\|u\|_{2}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> using a batch size of <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=16</annotation></semantics></math>. In each batch, we draw random initial conditions from <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}\sim\mathcal{U}([-1,1]^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}\sim\mathcal{U}([-1,1]^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and randomize the inertia matrix as <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{diag}(J)\sim\mathcal{U}([0.1,10]^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{diag}(J)\sim\mathcal{U}([0.1,10]^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. We compare the compute times on the backward and forward passes for this problem with <span id="A2.SS1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trajax</span> and <span id="A2.SS1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DiffMPC</span>. Results are reported in Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ B.1 Reinforcement Learning Benchmark ‣ Appendix B Results: Additional Details and Experiments ‣ Differentiable Model Predictive Controlon the GPU" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We note a significant speedup (approximately <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7.3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.3\times</annotation></semantics></math> for the backward pass) when solving this problem using <span id="A2.SS1.p5.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DiffMPC</span> compared to <span id="A2.SS1.p5.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trajax</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Imitation Learning Benchmark</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For this task, we consider the cart-pole problem in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amos et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, with states <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4})\in\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4})\in\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (m, m/s, rad, rad/s) and inputs <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (N). The model is parametrized with a length <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the cart mass <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pole mass <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the gravitational constant <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Its dynamics are</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E20.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{x}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{x}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=x_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=x_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E20.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{x}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{x}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{-m_{p}L\sin(x_{3})x_{4}^{2}+m_{p}g\sin(x_{3})\cos(x_{3})}{(M+m_{p}(1-\cos^{2}(x_{3}))L}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{-m_{p}L\sin(x_{3})x_{4}^{2}+m_{p}g\sin(x_{3})\cos(x_{3})}{(M+m_{p}(1-\cos^{2}(x_{3}))L},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E20.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{x}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{x}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=x_{4}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=x_{4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20c)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E20.4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{x}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{x}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{-m_{p}L\sin(x_{3})x_{4}+m_{p}g\sin(x_{3})\cos(x_{3})+u}{M+m_{p}(1-\cos^{2}(x_{3}))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{-m_{p}L\sin(x_{3})x_{4}+m_{p}g\sin(x_{3})\cos(x_{3})+u}{M+m_{p}(1-\cos^{2}(x_{3}))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20d)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and we let <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{c}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{c}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{p}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{p}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9.81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9.81</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=m_{p}+m_{c}=1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=m_{p}+m_{c}=1.1</annotation></semantics></math>. To generate expert data, we use <span id="A2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DiffMPC</span> in the form of <span id="A2.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OCP</span>, with the quadratic objective in <span id="A2.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QP</span></p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{t}=\mathrm{diag}(1,2,1.5,1),\quad\mathrm{and}\quad R_{t}=0.05\quad\forall t=0,...,T." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>and</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{t}=\mathrm{diag}(1,2,1.5,1),\quad\mathrm{and}\quad R_{t}=0.05\quad\forall t=0,...,T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Based on this expert policy, 32 different initial conditions <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=(x_{10},x_{20},x_{30},x_{40})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>20</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>30</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>40</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=(x_{10},x_{20},x_{30},x_{40})</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled according to the uniform distributions <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{10}\sim\mathcal{U}([-0.5,0.5])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{10}\sim\mathcal{U}([-0.5,0.5])</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{20}\sim\mathcal{U}([-0.5,0.5])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>20</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{20}\sim\mathcal{U}([-0.5,0.5])</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{30}\sim\mathcal{U}([-\pi,\pi])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>30</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{30}\sim\mathcal{U}([-\pi,\pi])</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{40}\sim\mathcal{U}([-1,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>40</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{40}\sim\mathcal{U}([-1,1])</annotation></semantics></math>. From each initial condition, an expert trajectory is generated using <span id="A2.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DiffMPC</span> with the costs in (<a href="#A2.E21" title="In B.2 Imitation Learning Benchmark ‣ Appendix B Results: Additional Details and Experiments ‣ Differentiable Model Predictive Controlon the GPU" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). For imitation learning, we learn the cost parameters <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=(Q_{11},Q_{22},Q_{33},Q_{44})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>33</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>44</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=(Q_{11},Q_{22},Q_{33},Q_{44})</annotation></semantics></math> and draw the initial parameters of the learned policy from a uniform distribution <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math>. Training is conducted using a batch size of 32, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amos et al. (2018)</a></cite>, by gradient descent using a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the mean-square-error objective defined in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.5 Reinforcement Learning and Imitation Learning ‣ 3 Differentiable Model Predictive Control on the GPU ‣ Differentiable Model Predictive Controlon the GPU" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Both <span id="A2.SS2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trajax</span> and <span id="A2.SS2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DiffMPC</span> were limited to five SQP and iLQR iterations and are warm-started using the expert trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Drifting Experiments</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vehicle model.</span> For the drifting experiments, we consider a dynamic bicycle model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lew et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for both planning and simulation. We model tire forces using a coupled slip brush Fiala model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Svendenius, 2007</a>)</cite>. In this setting, the state of the system is defined by <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(r,v,\beta,\omega_{r},\Delta\phi,e,s)\in\mathbb{R}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(r,v,\beta,\omega_{r},\Delta\phi,e,s)\in\mathbb{R}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (rad/s) is a yaw rate, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> (m/s) is a longitudinal velocity, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (rad) is a sideslip angle, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (rad/s) is the average rear wheel speeds, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\phi</annotation></semantics></math> (rad) is a heading tracking error with respect to a reference, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is a lateral tracking error to the same reference, and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (m) is the path distance traveled along the reference trajectory. The car is driven by the control input <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=(\delta,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(\delta,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (rad) is the steering angle, and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (Nm) is the engine torque. The simulator discretizes the vehicle’s dynamics using a high-accuracy Dormand-Prince method implemented in <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Diffrax</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kidger, 2021</a>)</cite>, whereas the MPC model uses a simple trapezoidal scheme discretized at <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> sec.
Box constraints on the control inputs are enforced in the simulator but omitted in the MPC controller so that robustness to actuator saturation is learned implicitly through training, and so that simpler unconstrained problems can be solved both during training and at runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The vehicle dynamics are parameterized by the parameters <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b,I_{z},m,r_{\mathrm{w}},I_{\mathrm{w}},C_{f},C_{r},\mu_{f},\mu_{r})\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b,I_{z},m,r_{\mathrm{w}},I_{\mathrm{w}},C_{f},C_{r},\mu_{f},\mu_{r})\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> define the location of the center of mass of the vehicle, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is its mass, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the wheel radius, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moment of inertia about the yaw axis, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moment of inertia of a wheel, and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the cornering stiffness and friction coefficients of the front and read wheels <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{f,r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{f,r\}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Nominal vehicle parameters are given in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.3 Drifting Experiments ‣ Appendix B Results: Additional Details and Experiments ‣ Differentiable Model Predictive Controlon the GPU" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Nominal vehicle model parameters and controller gains for the baseline MPC policy.</figcaption>
